# EWC3 Labs canonical line-ending policy: LF everywhere.
#
# WHY: CRLF<->LF drift produces phantom whole-file diffs, poisons `git blame`, and makes a clean
# checkout on another machine look modified. Git for Windows installs `core.autocrlf=true` at the
# SYSTEM level, so a fresh box reintroduces the problem unless something overrides it.
#
# Belts and suspenders — three independent layers, any one of which is sufficient:
#   1. global git config     core.autocrlf=false, core.eol=lf   (per user)
#   2. this file                                                (per repo, travels with the clone)
#   3. .editorconfig                                            (per repo, editor-side)
# All three means a fresh clone on a never-configured machine still behaves.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Deliberately NOT excepted: .sln, .csproj, .props, .targets, .ps1, .cmd, .bat.
# MSBuild, PowerShell, and modern cmd.exe all read LF fine. If a legacy batch file ever misbehaves
# (cmd.exe has historic edge cases with LF-only files around multi-line blocks and GOTO), add the
# NARROWEST possible exception here rather than reverting the policy:
#   *.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.7z    binary
*.wav   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.snk   binary
*.pfx   binary
*.p12   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.xlsm  binary
*.xlsb  binary
*.stl   binary
*.3mf   binary
*.one   binary
